        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Evaporative and ultrasonic humidifiers are both sold as cool mist, and on a store shelf they look interchangeable. Both sit on a dresser or the floor, both hold a gallon or more of water, and both promise relief from cracked lips, static shocks and a dry throat in the morning. Underneath, they work in completely different ways, and that difference shows up in your home within a week of use.
          </p>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier blows room air through a wet wick. Water leaves the wick as invisible vapor, and the minerals dissolved in your tap water stay behind in the wick. An ultrasonic humidifier uses a small plate vibrating at a very high frequency to shatter water into a fine visible mist. That mist carries whatever was dissolved in the water into the room, which is where the fine white dust on nearby surfaces comes from when the water is hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the choice usually comes down to your water and your tolerance for noise. Evaporative units are forgiving of hard tap water and cannot easily push a room past a comfortable humidity, but they make steady fan noise and need replacement wicks. Ultrasonic units are close to silent and need no wick, but they reward you for using distilled or very soft water and punish you with dust and a dirtier tank if you do not.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Evaporative</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ultrasonic</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How it adds moisture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fan pulls air through a wet wick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vibrating plate makes a fine mist</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">White dust with hard water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minimal; minerals stay in the wick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common unless water is distilled</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Steady fan sound, like a box fan on low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near silent; occasional gurgle</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ongoing cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replacement wicks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Distilled water or demineralizing cartridges</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over-humidifying risk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low; output slows as air gets moist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher without a working humidistat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Visible mist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Neither type is better in every room. A large living area in a house on hard well water is a strong case for evaporative. A nursery or bedroom where any fan hum keeps someone awake is a strong case for ultrasonic, as long as you are honest with yourself about buying distilled water. If you are still shopping for a specific model, our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier picks</a> cover both types, and the sections below explain each trade-off in turn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Each One Actually Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier is the oldest and simplest design. Water from the tank feeds a tray, and a wick, usually a paper or fiber pad, stands in that tray and soaks water up like a sponge. A fan pulls dry room air through the saturated wick. The air picks up moisture as it passes, and what comes out the top is slightly cooler, more humid air with no visible mist at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful quirk of that design is that it regulates itself. Evaporation is fastest when the surrounding air is dry and slows as the air gets wetter, so an evaporative unit naturally puts out less moisture as the room approaches a comfortable level. It is not a perfect humidistat, and a big machine in a small closed room can still overshoot, but it is much harder to soak a room with an evaporative humidifier than with a mist-based one.
          </p>

          <p>
            An ultrasonic humidifier has no wick and, in many models, no meaningful fan. A ceramic or metal disc called a transducer sits under a shallow pool of water and vibrates at an ultrasonic frequency, far above what you can hear. The vibration breaks the water surface into droplets a few microns across, and a small fan or simple convection carries that cool mist out of a nozzle. You can see it leave the machine, which many people find reassuring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because it is turning liquid water into droplets rather than evaporating it, an ultrasonic unit keeps producing at its set rate regardless of how humid the room already is. That is why the humidistat matters more on an ultrasonic machine. A good one with an accurate built-in sensor will shut the mist off at your target. A cheap one with no sensor will keep running until the tank is empty, and in a small bedroom that can mean condensation on cold windows by morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a third family worth knowing about: impeller humidifiers, which fling water off a spinning disc, and warm-mist or steam units, which boil water. Impeller models behave much like ultrasonics where minerals are concerned. Steam units sidestep the mineral problem because boiling leaves the minerals in the tank, at the cost of more electricity and a hot reservoir. We compare that side of the market in <a href="/warm-mist-vs-cool-mist-humidifier/">warm mist vs cool mist humidifiers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="white-dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. White Dust, Minerals and What the EPA Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            White dust is the single biggest practical difference between the two types. Tap water carries dissolved calcium, magnesium and other minerals, and hard water carries a lot of them. When an ultrasonic humidifier atomizes that water, the droplets evaporate in the air and leave the minerals behind as fine particles. Those particles settle as a white or gray film on furniture, electronics and dark floors, usually closest to the humidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not just a cleaning nuisance. The US Environmental Protection Agency's guidance on home humidifiers notes that ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers can disperse minerals and microorganisms from their tank water into the air, and that evaporative and steam humidifiers are generally less likely to do so. The agency's practical advice follows from that: use water with low mineral content, such as distilled or demineralized water, in mist-type units, and clean all humidifiers regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier avoids most of this because evaporation releases water vapor, not droplets. The minerals stay behind in the wick, which is exactly why wicks harden, turn crusty and eventually stop soaking up water. In a sense the wick is a disposable mineral filter. You pay for it in replacement pads instead of paying for it in dust and distilled water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ultrasonic makers have tried to solve the problem with demineralization cartridges and filter cassettes that sit in the water path. These can reduce white dust, especially with moderately hard water, but they have a limited capacity and need replacing on a schedule that depends heavily on how hard your water is. Owners on very hard water commonly report that cartridges help for a few weeks and then the dust returns. They are a mitigation, not a cure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ultrasonic plus hard tap water equals white dust.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your kettle scales up quickly or your shower glass spots, your water is hard enough to make an ultrasonic humidifier dust the room. Either commit to distilled water or buy an evaporative unit.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are unsure how hard your water is, a cheap test strip or your utility's annual water quality report will tell you. Anything above roughly seven grains per gallon, about 120 parts per million, is commonly classed as hard, and that is the range where white dust becomes hard to ignore with an ultrasonic machine. Soft municipal water and homes with a water softener sit in a gray zone, which we cover next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tap, Distilled or Filtered Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            Distilled water is the cleanest fix for an ultrasonic humidifier. Distillation removes nearly all dissolved minerals, so there is almost nothing to turn into dust, and the tank and transducer stay cleaner for longer. The catch is cost and hassle. A medium room humidifier can go through a gallon or more a day in dry weather, and at typical grocery prices that adds up to a noticeable monthly bill across a full winter, plus the chore of carrying jugs home.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pitcher filter is not a substitute. Most pitcher filters are designed to improve taste and reduce things like chlorine and some metals; they do little to the calcium and magnesium that make white dust. Our guide to <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitchers</a> explains what those filters are certified to reduce, and hardness is generally not on the list. A countertop distiller or reverse osmosis water is a different matter and works well if you already have one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Softened water is a common trap. A water softener swaps calcium and magnesium for sodium, so your water stops scaling pipes, but the total dissolved solids do not disappear. In an ultrasonic humidifier, softened water can still leave residue, just a different kind. It is usually better than hard water for white dust, but it is not equivalent to distilled.
          </p>

          <p>
            Evaporative humidifiers are far less fussy. Plain tap water is fine in most of them, and some makers specifically advise against distilled water for wick models because a small amount of mineral content helps the wick wick properly. Hard water shortens wick life rather than dusting your room, so the cost of hard water shows up as more frequent pad changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever type you own, never use water that has been sitting in the tank for days. Stale water is where bacteria and mold start, and the EPA guidance on humidifiers recommends emptying, drying and refilling tanks rather than topping them off. Fresh water every day, and a proper clean every few days, matters more than which water you start with. Our step by step guide on <a href="/how-to-clean-a-humidifier/">how to clean a humidifier</a> covers the routine for both types.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Noise and Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is where ultrasonic humidifiers win clearly. With no fan, or only a small low-speed one, a typical ultrasonic unit makes little more than a faint hiss and the occasional gurgle as water moves from the tank into the base. In a bedroom or nursery that near-silence is the main reason people choose one, and it is why so many of the models in our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifier for bedroom picks</a> are ultrasonic.
          </p>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier always has a fan, and the fan is how it does its work. On low it sounds like a quiet box fan, which many people find easy to sleep through or even soothing. On high, which is where it does most of its humidifying in a dry room, it is plainly audible. Larger console evaporative units for open-plan spaces are louder again. If you need a silent bedroom, evaporative is the harder choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electricity costs are low for both, and much lower than for warm-mist or steam units. Ultrasonic humidifiers typically draw in the range of 20 to 50 watts, and evaporative units draw a similar amount on low and more on their highest fan setting. Run around the clock, either type usually costs a few dollars a month in electricity at typical US rates, so energy should not decide the purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            The running costs that do differ are consumables. An evaporative unit needs a new wick every one to three months of steady use, sooner on hard water, and replacement pads commonly cost in the range of ten to twenty dollars each depending on brand and size. An ultrasonic unit needs no wick, but if you buy distilled water to avoid white dust, that becomes its consumable, and over a winter it can cost more than wicks do. Demineralizing cartridges sit in between.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges, Not Test Results</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-50 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical draw, either type</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-3 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical wick life</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Target indoor humidity</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Near 0 dB</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added fan noise, most ultrasonics</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The honest comparison, then, is a few dollars of electricity either way, plus wicks for evaporative or distilled water for ultrasonic on hard water. On soft water, an ultrasonic machine run on tap water is usually the cheapest humidifier to own. On hard water, evaporative usually wins once you count what you would spend keeping the dust down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wicks, Cleaning and Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every humidifier is a tank of still water, and still water grows things. Both types need regular cleaning, but they fail in different ways if you neglect them. Understanding those failure modes is more useful than any claim about one type being more hygienic than the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            An evaporative wick is a damp fiber pad that sits in water for weeks, and it can grow mold or bacteria if the unit is left full and switched off, or if the pad is used long past its life. A neglected wick usually announces itself with a musty or sour smell when the fan starts. Because the moisture leaving the machine is vapor, it is less able to carry microbes into the room than mist is, but a moldy pad is still something to replace, not rinse.
          </p>

          <p>
            An ultrasonic humidifier has no pad to grow mold on, which is part of its appeal, but its tank and transducer well can build up biofilm and scale. Because it sends droplets of tank water into the air, anything growing in that water has a direct path into the room. That is why the EPA guidance on humidifiers puts so much weight on daily emptying and regular disinfecting for mist-producing units in particular.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some ultrasonic models advertise UV lamps, antimicrobial tanks or self-cleaning cycles. These can help at the margins, but none of them replaces emptying the tank and cleaning it. Manufacturer claims about killing germs are worth reading carefully: a UV lamp only treats water that passes close to it, and a coated tank only slows growth on that surface. A wide-mouth top-fill tank that you can reach into and scrub is worth more than most of those features.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical routine for either type is to empty and refill with fresh water daily, wipe and dry the tank every few days, and descale and disinfect once a week following the manual. For evaporative units, add a wick change whenever the pad stiffens, discolors or smells. If you would rather not think about upkeep at all, an evaporative model with easy-to-find wicks is the more forgiving choice, because a crusty wick reduces output instead of dusting the room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with your water. If you are on hard water and will not buy distilled, choose evaporative. That one decision avoids white dust, keeps mineral particles out of the air and removes the main health caution the EPA raises about mist-type units. You will trade some fan noise and a recurring wick purchase for it, which most people find a fair exchange.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are on soft water, or you already use distilled or reverse osmosis water, an ultrasonic humidifier is the more pleasant machine to live with. It is quieter, has no wick to buy, and with the right water it leaves no dust. Choose one with a real humidistat and a tank you can open fully to clean.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best choice</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hard tap water, no distilled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evaporative</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minerals stay in the wick, not on your furniture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soft water or distilled on hand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultrasonic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quietest option with no consumable wick</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light sleeper, small bedroom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultrasonic with humidistat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No fan noise; sensor prevents overshoot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large open living area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evaporative console</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High output that self-limits as the room gets moist</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Home with infants or allergies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evaporative or steam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less dispersal of tank minerals and microbes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wants the least upkeep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evaporative</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neglect lowers output instead of dusting the room</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Room size matters too. Evaporative console units handle large spaces well because their output climbs with fan speed and slows naturally as the room approaches target humidity. Ultrasonic units for big rooms exist, but their white dust problem scales with output. For a living room or open-plan space, see our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-large-room/">best humidifier for large rooms</a>, and for whole-floor coverage it may be time to look at a furnace-mounted system instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, set a target rather than running either machine flat out. Indoor humidity of roughly 30 to 50 percent is the commonly recommended range, and the EPA advises keeping it below 60 percent to discourage mold and dust mites. A separate hygrometer costs little and tells you when you have reached it. Our guide to <a href="/ideal-indoor-humidity/">ideal indoor humidity</a> explains how to adjust that target for cold weather and condensation on windows.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running an ultrasonic on hard tap water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the classic cause of white dust on furniture and electronics. Ultrasonic units put whatever is dissolved in the water into the air. If your water scales a kettle quickly, either switch to distilled water or choose an evaporative model rather than wiping the room every week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping off old water instead of refilling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adding fresh water to a tank that has been sitting for days keeps the old, bacteria-friendly water in circulation. Empty the tank, let it dry if you can, and refill with fresh water daily. It costs nothing and does more for hygiene than any antimicrobial feature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping a wick far past its life</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hard, discolored or smelly wick evaporates less water and can harbor mold. Many owners keep running a dead pad and conclude the humidifier is too weak for the room. Replace wicks every one to three months, sooner on hard water, and keep a spare on hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a pitcher filter to stop white dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most pitcher filters target taste, chlorine and some metals, not the calcium and magnesium that create white dust. Using filtered water in an ultrasonic humidifier usually changes very little. Distilled or reverse osmosis water is what actually removes the minerals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running without a humidity target</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ultrasonic units without a working humidistat keep misting regardless of room conditions, which leads to condensation on windows and damp corners. Set a target between 30 and 50 percent, check it with a separate hygrometer, and turn the machine down if windows start to fog.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an evaporative or ultrasonic humidifier better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better everywhere. Evaporative humidifiers suit hard water, large rooms and people who want low upkeep, because minerals stay in the wick and output slows as the room gets humid. Ultrasonic humidifiers suit bedrooms and light sleepers because they are nearly silent, but they work best with distilled or very soft water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do evaporative humidifiers cause white dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very little. Evaporation releases water vapor and leaves dissolved minerals behind in the wick, which is why wicks harden and need replacing. White dust is mainly a problem with ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers, which turn tank water into droplets that carry minerals into the room as they dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are ultrasonic humidifiers safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are widely used and generally safe when maintained, but the EPA has noted that ultrasonic and impeller units can disperse minerals and microorganisms from tank water. Using distilled or demineralized water, emptying the tank daily and cleaning it regularly are the steps the EPA recommends to reduce that risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use tap water in an ultrasonic humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and on soft water it works fine. On hard water it usually produces white dust on nearby surfaces and scales the transducer faster. Demineralizing cartridges can reduce the dust for a while, but distilled or reverse osmosis water is the reliable fix if your tap water is hard.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is quieter, evaporative or ultrasonic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ultrasonic, by a clear margin. Most ultrasonic models have no fan or only a small one, so the main sound is a faint hiss and occasional gurgle. Evaporative units rely on a fan to push air through the wick, so they always make some steady noise, especially on their higher output settings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do evaporative humidifier wicks need replacing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Commonly every one to three months of regular use, and sooner with hard water. Replace the wick when it becomes stiff, crusty, discolored or starts to smell musty, or when the humidifier no longer raises the room humidity the way it did when new. Check the maker's guidance for your model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do filterless humidifiers need less maintenance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They skip the wick, but they still need regular cleaning. A filterless ultrasonic humidifier sends tank water into the air as mist, so a dirty tank matters more, not less. Daily refills with fresh water and a weekly descale and disinfect are still necessary to keep it hygienic.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

